Acute Viral Rhinopharyngitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Rhinorrhea, Nasal congestion, Sneezing

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-005
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting nasal congestion causing respiratory distress and sleep deprivation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting acute-onset, severe nasal obstruction leading to a sensation of smothering, persistent dry cough, and marked distress. Given the rapid escalation of respiratory discomfort and the potential for a developing lower respiratory tract exacerbation (e.g., reactive airway disease), an immediate on-site clinical assessment is required to evaluate airway patency, auscultate lung fields, and provide immediate pharmacological relief. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period presents an unacceptable risk of escalating respiratory distress and severe patient anxiety, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent after-hours MBS item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I can't breathe through my nose at all, and the congestion is making me panic every time I try to lie down.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 3 days prior with a mild, scratchy sore throat and general malaise. This evolved linearly over 48 hours into profuse, clear mucoid rhinorrhea and frequent bouts of sneezing. In the past 12 hours, the rhinorrhea has transitioned to severe nasal congestion and obstruction, accompanied by a relentless, dry, tickling cough that prevents any restorative sleep. Patient reports feeling feverish earlier in the day but denies frank rigors, chest pain, or purulent sputum.
Functional Status: Independent, but acutely fatigued due to sleep disruption.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High level of subjective distress driven by the sensation of obstructed breathing.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 90 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, speaking in full sentences but frequently mouth-breathing. Appears tired and visibly frustrated by the congestion.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Multiplex respiratory viral PCR panel detecting Rhinovirus, Coronavirus, or Adenovirus could assist in confirming the diagnosis if symptoms become atypical or prolonged, but is not clinically indicated at this acute juncture.
Physical Exam: Anterior rhinoscopy reveals deeply erythematous and markedly swollen nasal turbinates bilaterally, significantly occluding the nasal passages. Profuse clear mucoid secretions are visible. Oropharynx shows mild posterior erythema without exudate. Cervical lymph nodes are palpable but non-tender. Lung fields are definitively clear to auscultation bilaterally with equal air entry and zero wheeze or crepitations.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is heavily air-conditioned with dry air; a prominent pile of used tissues is on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has aggressively utilized over-the-counter saline sprays with negligible relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Acute Viral Rhinopharyngitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Allergic Rhinitis - Ruled out due to the lack of chronic allergic history, the presence of a viral prodrome (malaise, low-grade fever), and the definitive lack of intense nasal or ocular pruritus.
Influenza - Unlikely given the absence of sudden, severe systemic symptoms such as high-grade fevers, extreme prostration, and severe myalgia.
Acute Bacterial Sinusitis - Ruled out at this stage due to the short duration of illness (less than 10 days), the clear (rather than purulent) nature of the discharge, and the lack of severe localized unilateral facial pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
Telephone triage highlighted a patient experiencing severe, distressing nasal obstruction and a rapidly worsening cough, prompting fears of an asthmatic or lower respiratory complication. Urgent home assessment was clinically mandated to perform a thorough respiratory examination, definitively rule out lower airway pathology, and aggressively manage the upper airway obstruction to prevent further distress and fatigue. The physical exam confirmed an isolated upper respiratory viral infection. By intervening directly with potent topical decongestants and providing immediate clinical reassurance, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was averted. This assessment and intervention could not safely be delayed until standard consulting hours, fulfilling the requirements for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Oxymetazoline 0.05% nasal spray, 2 sprays per nostril strictly twice daily for a maximum of 3 days, to immediately reduce turbinate edema. Advised Paracetamol 1000mg four times daily as needed for malaise.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maximize the effectiveness of the nasal spray, blow your nose gently to clear gross mucus immediately before application. Aim the nozzle slightly outward towards the ear rather than the nasal septum; this ensures the medication coats the swollen turbinates directly and prevents mechanical trauma and bleeding from the delicate septal tissue.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 5-7 days if symptoms fail to improve or if purulent discharge develops.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing severe shortness of breath, chest pain, inability to swallow, or high fevers accompanied by a stiff neck.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on acute clinical presentation.
Clinical Course: The acute congestive phase typically peaks around days 3-5 and gradually resolves over 7-10 days. The post-viral dry cough may linger for up to 3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if you start coughing up dark green or blood-stained phlegm, develop severe localized facial pain, or experience true shortness of breath at rest.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The severe blockage is due to swollen tissue, not just trapped mucus. The prescribed medicated spray contains a strong vasoconstrictor that will rapidly shrink the blood vessels in the nose, opening the airway.
Actionable Steps: Strictly limit the medicated nasal spray to 3 days to prevent rebound congestion. Sleep with an extra pillow to elevate the head and utilize a cool-mist humidifier to counteract the drying effect of mouth-breathing.

